## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses understanding of bridging, threading, backpressure management, and lifecycle handling in a practical logging scenario.

## Key Concepts
- React Native bridge patterns (DirectEvent vs EventEmitter)
- Background thread file tailing with rotation detection
- Fixed-size buffering and batch emission to preserve ordering guarantees
- Backpressure strategies: dropping oldest versus dropping newest
- App lifecycle handling for background task management

## Usage Snippet
```javascript
const Logs = require('react-native-logs');

const logs = Logs.startLogStream({
  logFilePath: '/var/log/app.log',
  pollIntervalMs: 500,
  maxEntriesPerBatch: 100,
});

logs.onLog((entries) => {
  console.log('New log entries:', entries);
});

// Later, when done:
logs.stopLogStream();
```

## Trade-offs
| Strategy | Trade-off |
|----------|------------|
| Drop oldest | Lower memory floor; risk losing historical context |
| Drop newest | Preserve context; may miss recent critical errors |
| File rotation by inode | Reliable on most filesystems; requires platform-specific handling |
| File rotation by size | Simpler to implement; less robust |
| Pause on background | Lower power usage; may miss logs during suspension |
| Background task | Continuous logging; higher battery consumption |

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical React Native fetch patterns: cancellation prevents stale data and memory leaks during auto-refresh and manual refresh. It also checks hook design and safe state updates in unmount scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- AbortController usage in fetch calls
- useEffect cleanup and interval management
- Race condition avoidance with latest-request checks
- Small custom hook design for reusable cancellation logic

## Code Example

```javascript
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';

export function useCancelableFetch(url) {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const currentReq = useRef(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    let isActive = true;
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    const id = ++currentReq.current;
    setLoading(true);
    fetch(url, { signal: ctrl.signal })
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(d => {
        if (isActive && id === currentReq.current) {
          setData(d);
          setLoading(false);
        }
      })
      .catch(e => {
        if (isActive && e.name !== 'AbortError') setError(e);
        if (isActive) setLoading(false);
      });
    return () => {
      isActive = false;
      ctrl.abort();
    };
  }, [url]);

  return { data, loading, error };
}
```

